{"color":"DEFAULT","isTrashed":false,"isPinned":false,"isArchived":false,"annotations":[{"description":"","source":"DOCS","title":"incorporate this into the style guide and the mas...","url":"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1P7DE5VAHAYPWQKYnWm7nHPVb6pjYqloq4jIb9ZZ-rhI/edit?tab=t.0"}],"textContent":"Excellent, let's integrate these automation strategies directly into your master style guide and template.\n\n**Updated Master Style Guide and Template:**\n\n```\n[MEMOIR PROJECT] MASTER STYLE GUIDE\n\n**I. Core Style Elements:**\n\n* Maintain darkly humorous, self-aware tone throughout.\n* Use second-person address to directly engage readers.\n* Incorporate meta-awareness about narrative reliability.\n* Employ vivid metaphors for mental states.\n* Balance raw vulnerability with sardonic humor.\n\n**II. Family Structure (IMPORTANT):**\n\n* Two Fathers (primary weekday household):\n    * Father #1: Much older (30+ years senior to partner), structured, stable.\n    * Father #2: Much younger, biological father, \"less than a generation apart\" from narrator, unpredictable.\n* Two Mothers (weekend visitation):\n    * Mother #1: Biological mother, \"favorite\" parent, weekend visits only, permissive.\n    * Mother #2: Mother's partner, part of special weekend dynamic.\n* Extended Family:\n    * Grandfather: Indulgent figure who \"couldn't say no\" (previously grandmother in original).\n    * Victor Dominic: Uncle who died in his 30s.\n    * Alexander Scott: Father #2's abusive ex-partner.\n    * Seth Braden: \"The young guy\" - Father #2's boyfriend after Alexander Scott.\n    * Michael William: Stabilizing stepparent who created needed structure.\n\n**III. Name Replacements:**\n\n1.  Charlie → Alexander Scott\n2.  Mark → Seth Braden\n3.  Harry → Michael William\n4.  Buchi → Victor Dominic\n5.  Grindr → Flicker (dating app)\n6.  Kaiser → Summit Health (healthcare provider)\n\n**IV. Location & Time Redactions:**\n\n* Ohio → \"a Midwestern state\"\n* Specific ages → generalized time periods\n* Specific years → relational time markers\n* Specific medication dosages → general descriptions\n* Childhood home details → \"outdated carpeting and dizzying geometric wallpaper\"\n\n**V. Key Themes:**\n\n1.  Reality vs. Perception: Visual hallucinations, paranoia, verification systems.\n2.  Structure and Routine as Survival: Need for predictability and control.\n3.  Medication Journey: Trial and error, partial effectiveness, stigma.\n4.  Memory as Gift and Curse: Photographic memory complicating reality.\n5.  Family Patterns: Genetic components, chaotic upbringing affecting adult relationships.\n\n**VI. Character Impression Tracking (Automation Enhanced):**\n\n* **Spreadsheet Integration:**\n    * Create a dedicated spreadsheet (Google Sheets or Excel) titled \"Character Impression Tracking.\"\n    * Use the following column headers: \"Original Name,\" \"Current Name,\" \"Early Memoir Impression,\" \"Later Memoir Impression,\" \"Narrative Purpose,\" and \"Notes.\"\n    * Implement data validation for \"Early Memoir Impression,\" \"Later Memoir Impression,\" and \"Narrative Purpose\" using dropdown lists to ensure consistent terminology.\n    * Apply conditional formatting to highlight significant changes in character impressions or specific keywords in notes.\n    * Utilize filters and sorting for efficient data retrieval.\n    * Use the comment feature for detailed notes and examples.\n* **Document Linking:**\n    * In your memoir document, create hyperlinks from character names to their corresponding rows in the \"Character Impression Tracking\" spreadsheet.\n    * Maintain consistent file and tab naming conventions for easy cross-referencing.\n* **Digital Note-Taking:**\n    * Use a digital note-taking tool (Evernote, OneNote, Obsidian) for character-related notes, tagged and searchable.\n    * Utilize full-text search within your memoir document and note-taking tool.\n* **Regular Review:**\n    * Schedule periodic reviews of the \"Character Impression Tracking\" spreadsheet.\n    * Use version control for both the memoir and spreadsheet to track changes.\n\n**VII. SPECIFIC INSTRUCTIONS (Template Area - To Be Filled Per Task):**\n\n* [ADD DETAILED INSTRUCTIONS FOR THIS PARTICULAR REQUEST - e.g.:\n    * Check for instances of \"Charlie\" and replace with \"Alexander Scott.\"\n    * Ensure the mother/father relationships match the revised family structure.\n    * Maintain the darkly humorous tone throughout.\n    * Keep references to \"shadows in periphery\" consistent.\n    * Update the character tracking spread sheet.\n    * etc.]\n\n**VIII. MEMOIR EXCERPT (Template Area - To Be Filled Per Task):**\n\n* [PASTE MEMOIR SECTION HERE]\n```\n\n**Key Changes and Additions:**\n\n* **Section VI: Character Impression Tracking (Automation Enhanced):**\n    * This section now explicitly outlines the spreadsheet integration, document linking, digital note-taking, and regular review strategies.\n    * It provides specific instructions on how to set up the spreadsheet and use its features effectively.\n* **Template Areas:**\n    * Sections VII (SPECIFIC INSTRUCTIONS) and VIII (MEMOIR EXCERPT) remain as template areas to be filled with task-specific information.\n\nThis updated master style guide and template now serves as a comprehensive tool for maintaining consistency and efficiency throughout your memoir project.\n\n","title":"","userEditedTimestampUsec":1742671440314000,"createdTimestampUsec":1742671437833000,"textContentHtml":"<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.38;margin-top:0.0pt;margin-bottom:0.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:7.2pt;font-family:'Google Sans';color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\">Excellent, let's integrate these automation strategies directly into your master style guide and template.<\/span><\/p><br /><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.38;margin-top:0.